# Coding Style
- Try to separate the concerns when there are more than 500 lines of code; if impossible, use `#region` to separate the concerns (e.g: `Life Cycle`, `Public Methods`, `Helpers`, `Editor` etc.).
- Use braces in control statements unless they are simple single-line guard statements (`if (x) return;`, `if (x) continue;`).
- Implicit private in variables.
- Use `UniTask` instead of coroutine to perform asynchronous programming.
- During code review / code editing, prevent any warnings or errors in Rider Editor and Unity Editor.
- Use `#nullable enable` for scripts.

# GameObject
- Prefer parent-driven setup via `Initialize(...)` (or similar) over `Start` for cross-component wiring.
- `Awake`/`Start` are OK for self-contained setup (e.g. singleton registration, disabling editor-only objects).
- Do not rely on `Start` execution order across sibling components when a parent can wire dependencies explicitly.
- Avoid asynchronous initialization unless we want to optimize initialization time.
- For simple inspector behaviour on runtime components, use `NaughtyAttributes` instead of a custom Editor script.

# Compile Errors
- For first time installation with compile errors due to `GlobalOptions`, fix by changing build profile to `Andoird`.
- Statically check whether there are compile errors during local build by ensuring there are no editor-only code (`NaughtyAttributes`, `NUnit` etc.) shipped.

